```apex
/**
 * When an Account's BillingCountry changes, propagate the new value to the
 * MailingCountry of the account's contacts whose MailingCountry is blank
 * (never set) or still equals the *old* BillingCountry.  Contacts that have
 * a different (e.g. foreign) MailingCountry are left untouched.
 *
 * Designed for the MDM integration that upserts accounts in batches of 200.
 * One SOQL per trigger execution; no queries in loops.
 */
public class AccountCountrySync {

    public static void handle() {
        Map<Id, Account> oldMap = Trigger.oldMap;
        Map<Id, Account> newMap = Trigger.newMap;

        // ── 1. Identify the accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed ──
        Set<Id>   changedAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryById = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> newCountryById = new Map<Id, String>();

        for (Id accId : newMap.keySet()) {
            String oldC = oldMap.get(accId).BillingCountry;
            String newC = newMap.get(accId).BillingCountry;
            if (oldC != newC) {
                changedAccountIds.add(accId);
                oldCountryById.put(accId, oldC);
                newCountryById.put(accId, newC);
            }
        }

        // Nothing to do if no country changed.
        if (changedAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // ── 2. Single SOQL – only the contacts that belong to changed accounts ──
        List<Contact> relatedContacts = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
        ];

        // ── 3. Pick the contacts that should follow the account ──
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact c : relatedContacts) {
            String oldC = oldCountryById.get(c.AccountId);
            String newC = newCountryById.get(c.AccountId);

            // Update when MailingCountry is blank/never-set,
            // or still equals the account's *previous* BillingCountry.
            if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry) || c.MailingCountry == oldC) {
                c.MailingCountry = newC;
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        // ── 4. One DML call for all eligible contacts ──
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            Database.update(toUpdate, false);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key design decisions

| Concern | Decision |
|---|---|
| **Bulk safety** | One SOQL (scoped to the changed accounts), one `Database.update` for all contacts. No per-record queries or DML. |
| **`String.isBlank` vs `== null`** | `String.isBlank` covers both `null` and `''`, which is the safe check for a text field. |
| **`null` vs `null` comparison** | In Apex `null == null` is `true`, so a contact whose MailingCountry is null *and* whose account's old country was null would match the second branch. That's harmless because the first branch (`isBlank`) already catches it, and we only add the contact to the list once. |
| **`Database.update(list, false)`** | Allows partial failure (e.g. a validation rule on one contact) without aborting the entire 200-account batch. The trigger caller's transaction will still commit the successful rows. |
| **No `without sharing`** | The handler runs in the triggering user's context. The MDM integration user must have read/write on the contacts it needs to update—which is the same access it already needs to update the accounts. Adding `without sharing` would widen the blast radius without a clear benefit here. |
| **`Trigger.newMap` / `Trigger.oldMap`** | Used instead of lists so we get O(1) look-up of old values by Account Id, and we skip accounts whose BillingCountry was re-saved to the same value. |
